{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"responsive-undamaged-auklet","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nMost plan-review criteria have no eval fixture, so a rubric edit ships with no behavioral\noracle. The material to build one is already committed and free to read: the manifest\n`docs/experiments/plan-review-gate/replay/corpus-2026-08-30.manifest.json` records 6,482\nsidecar rows with `verified_count` 6,202, and `plan_replay/corpus.py` reconstructs each\nrow's at-review plan text into a `description` field (`corpus.py:270-288`).\n\nTurning that into fixtures is not a dump, because the corpus records what the reviewer\nsaid rather than what was true, and two hazards make naive mining wrong:\n\n1. **Reviewer noise.** The store holds 551 consecutive review pairs over byte-identical\n   material of which 208 flipped verdict, so a single observed finding is a noisy\n   datapoint. A fixture built from one would flip under that same noise.\n2. **Prompt vintage.** Criterion rubrics under `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/` were rewritten\n   repeatedly inside the corpus window, and `plan_review_verifier.md` last changed\n   2026-08-11. A finding produced by a since-retired rubric does not describe today's\n   criterion. Nothing in the existing eval code cohorts by prompt vintage: `regver`\n   (`manifest.py:139-185`) hashes the routing and criterion id sets but not rubric bytes,\n   and `verified_at_sha` is stamped on sidecars yet read by no code under\n   `src/rebar/llm/evals/`.\n\n## Approach\n\nAn offline miner that emits CANDIDATE fixture specs to a working directory for the sibling\nadmission runner. It writes nothing into `.rebar/evals/` and issues no model call, so it can\nbe re-run freely while its selection rules are tuned.\n\n**Vintage gate.** A review is eligible for criterion C only when its `review_event_ts`\n(`corpus.py:273`) postdates the last `origin/main` commit touching\n`src/rebar/llm/reviewers/plan_review_<C>.md`. A signal derived from the stored Pass-2 answer\nvector additionally requires postdating the last commit to `plan_review_verifier.md`.\n\n**Signals**, all offline and all independent of the reviewer's own verdict:\n- *Reproduction consensus* — the criterion fired, or stayed silent, consistently across two\n  or more reviews whose `material_fingerprint` values are equal.\n- *Author response* — `finding_survival == \"resolved_by_author\"` (`labels.py:158-171`), the\n  only per-finding label the labels module produces.\n- *Escaped defect, used positively only* — a ticket that passed review and then took a\n  `plan_defect` close or an inbound `caused_by` link (`labels.py:70-73`) yields a must-fire\n  candidate. Its absence is never treated as evidence of correct silence, so `clean_close`\n  (`labels.py:43`) is excluded as a must-not-fire source; must-not-fire candidates come from\n  reproduction consensus in the negative direction.\n\n**Tiered admission bar.** A candidate is proposed BLOCKING only when all four hold:\nreproduction consensus, author-response corroboration for a fire case, `abs(decision_margin)`\n(`sidecar.py:344-367`) at or above the module constant `MIN_MARGIN = 0.15`, and the vintage\ngate. Any single signal plus the vintage gate proposes an ADVISORY candidate. The margin\nconstant excludes findings sitting close enough to `block_threshold` that a small scoring\nperturbation would flip them, which is exactly the population that would make a fixture\nflaky; it is a named constant, and re-tuning it costs only another offline run.\n\n**Emitted shape.** Per criterion at most 8 cases, targeting 4 `expect: finding` and 4\n`expect: pass`, satisfying the balance rule at `eval.py:252-255`, plus a non-empty\n`gold_set` (`eval.py:265-267`).\n\n**Skip list.** A criterion with an open ticket titled `unreliable-criterion: <criterion-id>`\nis skipped, so the sibling self-healing loop cannot spend repeatedly on a criterion already\nrecorded as unminable.\n\n## Scope\n\nA new module under `src/rebar/llm/evals/` holding the miner and its selection predicates,\nconsuming `plan_replay/corpus.py` and `plan_replay/labels.py`. It covers only criteria that\n`criteria.effective_routing('.', gate_key='plan_review')` reports and for which no spec file\nexists under `.rebar/evals/` or `src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/`.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- Writing into `.rebar/evals/`, or admitting any candidate. The sibling admission runner\n  owns both.\n- Any model call.\n- Mining a criterion that already has a spec: `eval.py:69-78` resolves a project spec as a\n  replacement, so a mined file there would delete the packaged cases.\n- Per-finding true/false-positive labelling or human adjudication.\n\n## Testing\n\nUnit tests over the selection predicates using synthetic corpus rows, covering the vintage\nboundary, each of the four blocking conjuncts in isolation, the `clean_close` exclusion, the\nskip list, and strict spec validity of the emitted YAML. One integration test runs the miner\nover the real committed corpus with no model configured, asserting determinism across two\nruns. Every assertion is deterministic because no model is involved.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A review timestamped before its criterion's rubric commit is excluded while an\n      otherwise-identical review timestamped after it is included; verified by `pytest`\n      asserting the candidate set contains the second row's id and not the first's.\n- [ ] A corpus fixture whose only no-fire signal is a clean close produces zero must-not-fire\n      candidates; verified by `pytest` asserting an empty `expect: pass` case list.\n- [ ] Each of the four blocking conjuncts, removed one at a time, downgrades the candidate to\n      `advisory`, and the all-present case yields `blocking`; verified by a parameterized\n      `pytest` over the five cases asserting the emitted tier string.\n- [ ] Every emitted spec passes `validate_eval_spec(spec, strict=True)` with zero returned\n      errors and carries at most 8 cases, at least one `expect: finding`, at least one\n      `expect: pass`, and a non-empty `gold_set`; verified by `pytest` invoking the real\n      validator over each emitted file.\n- [ ] A criterion with an open `unreliable-criterion: <id>` ticket is absent from the emitted\n      candidate set; verified by `pytest` with a stubbed ticket listing asserting that id is\n      not present.\n- [ ] Two consecutive real runs over the committed corpus emit byte-identical output, and the\n      run prints a per-criterion candidate count; verified by `diff` of the two output trees\n      exiting 0.\n- [ ] The miner completes with the repository's network guard active, showing it issues no\n      model or network call; verified by `pytest` running it under that guard.\n\n## Definition of Done\n\n`make lint`, `make typecheck` and `make test` pass; the real-corpus run completes; the diff\ntouches no file under `.rebar/evals/` or `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/`.\n\n## Budget\n\n**$0.** Mining is a pure function of the frozen corpus and git metadata.","id":"549b-9cc5-940e-46c3","parent_id":"65dd-e93b-666a-4caa","priority":2,"tags":["plan-review","evals","fixtures"],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Mine per-criterion fire/no-fire fixture candidates from the plan-review sidecar corpus"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788318381961839586,"uuid":"d3f6c5c7-6acf-43d0-b6dc-d1cadd766ffd"}